.TH DOTS\-DECRYPT 1 "2026-08-28" "dots" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dots\-decrypt \- decrypt age artifacts to their target locations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dots\-decrypt
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dots\-decrypt
is the shell script
.IR scripts/decrypt.sh ,
invoked as
.B ./scripts/decrypt.sh
from inside a checkout of the
.B dots
repository, or as
.B just decrypt
(via
.BR Justfile ) .
It takes no command line arguments; any arguments given are ignored.  All
behavior is controlled by the environment variables described under
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.PP
Every secret in the repository is stored under
.I secrets/
as two age artifacts encrypting the same plaintext:
.TP
.I X.age
encrypted to the recipients listed in
.IR secrets/recipients.txt ;
decrypted without prompts on an established machine that holds the
matching private age identity.
.TP
.I X.phrase.age
passphrase-encrypted fallback for a fresh machine that has no identity
yet (the curl | bash bootstrap); age prompts for the passphrase.
.PP
The script walks
.IR secrets/ ,
finds every
.I *.age
file, and decrypts each one to a target file in the home directory.  The
target is chosen by the first path component below
.IR secrets/ :
.\" keep next five lines together
.TP
.I secrets/ssh/X.age
decrypts to
.IR ~/.ssh/X .
.TP
.I secrets/tokens/X.age
decrypts to
.IR ~/.config/tokens/X .
.\" end list
.PP
For each artifact the script tries, in order:
.TP
1.
the age identity (if one exists on this machine) against
.BR X.age ;
.TP
2.
the passphrase against
.BR X.phrase.age ;
.TP
3.
the passphrase against
.B X.age
itself \(em this covers legacy secrets stored as a single
passphrase-only artifact.
.\" end list
.PP
Each decryption is written to a temporary file created in the target
directory, the temporary file is set to mode 0600 with
.BR chmod (1),
and only then is it renamed over the target.  If
.BR age (1)
fails, the temporary file is removed and the existing target is left
untouched.  The target directory is created with
.B mkdir \-p
if needed and is set to mode 0700 before anything is decrypted into it.
.PP
Unless
.B FORCE
is set, an artifact whose target already exists and is strictly newer than
both artifacts is skipped entirely.  Concretely, decryption is skipped when
the target exists, the target is newer than
.BR X.age ,
and there is no passphrase copy, or the target is also newer than
.BR X.phrase.age .
.SH OPTIONS
.B dots\-decrypt
accepts no options or flags.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AGE_IDENTITY
Path to the age identity file.  Defaults to
.IR ~/.age/keys.txt .
If that file does not exist the script prints a note and relies on the
passphrase fallback instead.
.TP
.B FORCE
Set to
.B true
to re-decrypt even when the freshness check in
.B DESCRIPTION
would skip the artifact.  Any other value (including unset) leaves the
check active.
.TP
.B HOME
Used to resolve the default identity path
.RI ( ~/.age/keys.txt )
and every target directory
.RI ( ~/.ssh ,
.IR ~/.config/tokens ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All artifacts were processed.  This includes the cases where no
.I .age
files were found at all and where every target was skipped as up to date.
.TP
.B other than 0
The script runs with
.B set \-e
and stops at the first failure, with status 1.  This is the case when
.B age
is not installed, a decryption failed (wrong passphrase, aborted prompt,
corrupt artifact), a rename failed, or no artifact existed for a
.RI .age
file; the exit status of
.BR age (1)
itself is swallowed and not propagated.  Artifacts later in the
.I secrets/
tree are not processed and targets already written stay in place.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I scripts/decrypt.sh
The script itself; it derives
.I secrets/
from its own location, so the working directory is irrelevant.  The only
requirement is that the script file itself live in a checkout of the
repository (for example invoked by absolute path, as
.BR dots\-setup (1)
does).
.TP
.IR "secrets/*.age" " and " "secrets/*/*.age"
Encrypted artifacts.  Passphrase copies
.RI ( X.phrase.age )
are recognized and skipped as primary artifacts.  Artifacts in
subdirectories with no target mapping are warned about and skipped.
.TP
.I ~/.age/keys.txt
Default age identity, generated with
.BR "age-keygen \-o ~/.age/keys.txt" .
The corresponding public key belongs in
.IR secrets/recipients.txt .
.TP
.IR "~/.ssh/" " and " "~/.config/tokens/"
Target directories for the
.I ssh/
and
.I tokens/
secret subdirectories.  Created with
.B mkdir \-p
if missing, and forced to mode 0700 with
.B chmod 700
before every artifact that is actually decrypted into them \(em an
existing directory with looser permissions is silently tightened, and a
run in which every target is skipped as up to date leaves the mode
alone.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B "Error: age is not installed."
The
.B age
binary is not on
.BR PATH .
Exit status 1; nothing is decrypted.
.TP
.B "Using age identity: \fIpath\fR"
An identity file exists at
.B AGE_IDENTITY
and will be tried first.
.TP
.B "No age identity found at \fIpath\fR \(em will prompt for passphrase."
No identity file exists; every decryption goes via the passphrase
fallback.
.TP
.B "Warning: No target mapping for subdir '\fIsubdir\fR', skipping \fIrelpath\fR"
The artifact lives in a subdirectory of
.I secrets/
other than
.I ssh/
or
.IR tokens/ .
It is skipped; this is a warning, not an error.
.TP
.B "Skipping (up to date): \fIrelpath\fR -> \fItarget\fR"
The freshness check passed; the target was not re-decrypted.
.TP
.B "Decrypting: \fIrelpath\fR -> \fItarget\fR"
Decryption of this artifact starts.
.TP
.B "Identity did not match, falling back to passphrase..."
The age identity exists but could not decrypt
.B X.age
(for example after the key was rotated); the passphrase copy is tried
next.
.TP
.B "Error: passphrase decryption failed for \fIrelpath\fR"
Neither the identity nor the passphrase could decrypt the artifact (a
wrong passphrase, an aborted prompt, or a corrupt file).  The script exits
non-zero here; rest of the tree is not processed.
.TP
.B "Error: no artifacts found for \fIrelpath\fR"
Neither
.B X.age
nor
.B X.phrase.age
exists on disk.
.TP
.B "No .age files found in \fIsecrets-dir\fR"
.B find
matched nothing, for example because the checkout has no
.I secrets/
directory.  This is not an error; the exit status is 0.
.TP
.B "Decrypted \fIn\fR file(s)."
Number of
.I .age
files seen, including passphrase copies that were skipped without being
decrypted on their own.
.SH EXAMPLES
Decrypt all secrets on an established machine that has
.IR ~/.age/keys.txt :
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ./scripts/decrypt.sh
Using age identity: /Users/jlambert/.age/keys.txt
Decrypting: ssh/xrq.io-ecdsa -> /Users/jlambert/.ssh/xrq.io-ecdsa
Decrypted 2 file(s).
.fi
.RE
.PP
Fresh machine with no identity (the passphrase of the
.I *.phrase.age
fallback is asked for interactively):
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ./scripts/decrypt.sh
No age identity found at /home/alice/.age/keys.txt \(em will prompt for passphrase.
Decrypting: ssh/id_ed25519 -> /home/alice/.ssh/id_ed25519
[age prompts for the passphrase here]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Force re-decryption of everything, ignoring timestamps:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ FORCE=true ./scripts/decrypt.sh
.fi
.RE
.PP
Same effect, through
.BR just (1):
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ just decrypt
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dots\-setup (1),
.BR dots\-encrypt (1),
.BR dots\-verify (1),
.BR age (1)